      \field{abstract}{This study addresses the issue of social loafing among students at Muhammadiyah University of Sidoarjo, where individuals may feel demotivated due to their perceived insignificance in group contributions or the lack of correlation between contribution size and rewards. The aim is to explore the relationship between group cohesiveness and social loafing among these students. Utilizing a correlational quantitative design, data was gathered from 372 active students using a Likert scale. The analysis employed product-moment correlation via SPSS version 25.0. Results reveal a significant correlation between group cohesiveness and social loafing, indicating that cohesive groups may experience less social loafing. These findings underscore the importance of fostering group cohesion to mitigate social loafing tendencies among university students, with potential implications for enhancing group productivity and engagement in academic settings.}
